.TH NREAD 1 "November 2026" "nread 9.3"
.SH NAME
nread \- read text files in a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nread
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [[ +LINE [, COLUMN ]] " FILE" ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
nread is a read-only terminal text reader derived from GNU nano.
It supports navigation, searching, syntax highlighting, soft wrapping,
and multiple open files. Typing and pasting never alter the text.
There are no editing, saving, replacement, or command-execution commands.
With no filenames, piped or redirected standard input is read automatically.
Use a filename of \fB-\fR to read standard input explicitly.
Input is read directly into memory without a temporary file.
A controlling terminal is required for interactive keystrokes.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
Search for text. Alt+W repeats the search.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Open another file for reading.
.TP
.B Alt+, / Alt+.
Switch between open files.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X
Close the current file, or exit when it is the last file.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
Show help and the complete shortcut list.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -l , " --linenumbers"
Show line numbers.
.TP
.BR -S , " --softwrap"
Display long lines across multiple screen rows.
.TP
.BR -I , " --ignorercfiles"
Ignore nanorc configuration files.
.TP
.BR -f " FILE", " --rcfile=FILE"
Use only the specified configuration file.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show all available options.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show version information.
.SH CONFIGURATION
nread supports nano's nanorc configuration format for display settings,
syntax highlighting, and reader key bindings.
It looks for nreadrc in the configured system location, then /etc.
If neither exists it looks for nanorc in those same locations.
For user configuration it prefers ~/.nreadrc, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nread/nreadrc,
or ~/.config/nread/nreadrc; if none exists it uses ~/.nanorc,
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nano/nanorc, or ~/.config/nano/nanorc.
Only the first user configuration found is read.
Settings and bindings for disabled features are silently ignored.
Ignored bindings preserve existing reader shortcuts. Unknown settings
still report errors. Optional search and cursor-position histories can
be enabled with \fB-H\fR and \fB-P\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nanorc (5)
